#
# One answer to "what is a line ending in this repository", so that two gits
# looking at the same working tree agree about it.
#
# Without this the answer comes from core.autocrlf, which is a property of the
# git installation rather than of the project: Git for Windows sets it in its
# system config, a git on Linux does not.  A tree checked out by one then reads
# as wholly modified to the other - every line of every file, insertions exactly
# equal to deletions - and a commit made from the other side puts CRLF into the
# history for good.
#
#   text=auto  git decides what is text and normalises it to LF on the way in,
#              so a CRLF working file still hashes to the LF blob it came from.
#              Adding this file therefore dirties nothing that was already
#              correct: it only stops the two sides disagreeing.
#   eol=lf     and it is written back out as LF on checkout on every platform,
#              overriding autocrlf.  Which is what a .sh with a `#!` line needs,
#              and a workflow `run:` block is a shell script too.
* text=auto eol=lf
